WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.408
Welcome to part two.

00:00:01.638 --> 00:00:09.233
Mountains rise into peaks, but a plateau is an uplifted area with a broad, fairly level top and often steep edges.

00:00:09.463 --> 00:00:12.663
It is sometimes called a tableland or a plain in the air.

00:00:12.893 --> 00:00:18.781
On a cross-section, look for height plus a flat top; flatness alone would describe a plain as well.

00:00:19.531 --> 00:00:24.011
The Tibetan, East African and Colorado Plateaus are world examples.

00:00:24.241 --> 00:00:27.931
In India, the Deccan and Chota Nagpur Plateaus are important.

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:34.455
The chapter describes the Deccan as a large fertile region, while Chota Nagpur is known for mineral wealth.

00:00:34.685 --> 00:00:39.741
Plateaus can support farming, mining, grazing and settlement in different places.

00:00:40.491 --> 00:00:45.568
To describe a plateau properly, state its shape, its relative height, and one example.

00:00:45.798 --> 00:00:54.694
Then ask how it formed, because the chapter divides plateaus in five ways: tectonic, volcanic, dissected, intermontane and piedmont.

00:00:54.924 --> 00:01:01.281
The categories use formation, appearance and location, so they are not five identical kinds of label.

00:01:02.781 --> 00:01:08.243
A tectonic plateau forms when movements within Earth's crust raise a broad section of land.

00:01:08.473 --> 00:01:13.166
Imagine a wide block lifting while its upper surface remains relatively level.

00:01:13.396 --> 00:01:19.817
The textbook names the South African Plateau and the Turkish-Iranian Plateau as examples of this type.

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:22.871
A volcanic plateau forms differently.

00:01:23.101 --> 00:01:26.408
Lava spreads over a broad region in repeated flows.

00:01:26.638 --> 00:01:31.694
Each layer cools and hardens; many layers can make a high, extensive tableland.

00:01:31.924 --> 00:01:37.812
That is different from a volcanic mountain, where erupted material piles up around a vent into a cone.

00:01:38.562 --> 00:01:46.647
The chapter gives the Columbia Plateau in North America and parts of the Deccan and Malwa Plateaus in India as volcanic examples.

00:01:46.877 --> 00:01:53.469
If an exam asks you to compare tectonic and volcanic plateaus, write the process first, then an example.

00:01:53.699 --> 00:01:58.435
Uplift of a crustal area is one process; stacked lava flows are the other.

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:03.772
What builds a volcanic plateau: one tall cone or broad layers of lava?

00:02:12.002 --> 00:02:15.138
Broad successive layers of lava that cool and harden.

00:02:16.118 --> 00:02:20.897
A dissected plateau began as a raised surface but has been cut by erosion.

00:02:21.127 --> 00:02:26.887
Rivers can carve canyons, gorges and steep valleys into it, leaving an irregular surface.

00:02:27.117 --> 00:02:33.538
The Colorado Plateau, crossed by the Grand Canyon, and the Brazilian Plateau are the chapter's examples.

00:02:33.768 --> 00:02:37.203
Dissected describes what erosion has done to the surface.

00:02:37.953 --> 00:02:40.214
Intermontane means between mountains.

00:02:40.444 --> 00:02:43.004
Such a plateau is enclosed by ranges.

00:02:43.234 --> 00:02:50.402
The textbook's main example is the Tibetan Plateau, with the Himalayas to its south and the Kunlun Mountains to its north.

00:02:50.632 --> 00:02:55.475
Use a simple map or cross-section to show the plateau lying between the ranges.

00:02:56.225 --> 00:02:58.529
Piedmont refers to the foot of a mountain.

00:02:58.759 --> 00:03:03.729
A piedmont plateau lies between a mountain region and a lower plain or ocean.

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:09.293
The chapter names Patagonia in South America and the Piedmont Plateau of North America.

00:03:09.523 --> 00:03:19.379
Compare all three labels: dissected describes cutting by erosion, intermontane describes enclosure, and piedmont describes position at the mountain foot.

00:03:20.879 --> 00:03:24.249
A basin is a depressed area surrounded by higher land.

00:03:24.479 --> 00:03:29.279
Some basins collect rivers that do not reach the sea; this is inland drainage.

00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:35.760
The Tarim and Tsaidam Basins in Asia and the Chad Basin in Africa are examples in the chapter.

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:39.638
On a map, a basin is an area, not just one narrow channel.

00:03:40.388 --> 00:03:43.204
A valley is low land between hills or mountains.

00:03:43.434 --> 00:03:46.015
Some valleys contain rivers; some do not.

00:03:46.245 --> 00:03:52.624
Erosion by running water or moving ice can cut valleys, while movement of the crust can create a rift valley.

00:03:52.854 --> 00:03:58.507
To identify the cause, study the cross-section and any nearby faults or glacier evidence.

00:03:59.257 --> 00:04:03.631
Both basin and valley describe low ground, but shape and setting differ.

00:04:03.861 --> 00:04:07.317
A basin is a broader depression enclosed by higher land.

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:10.043
A valley is usually a long low corridor.

00:04:10.273 --> 00:04:18.059
Explain the difference aloud and draw both from the side; a labelled drawing will help you remember far better than a bare definition.

00:04:18.809 --> 00:04:22.457
Which is a broad enclosed depression: a basin or a valley?

00:04:30.687 --> 00:04:31.711
A basin.

00:04:31.941 --> 00:04:34.757
A valley is a low corridor between higher land.

00:04:35.737 --> 00:04:39.236
A river can cut downward into its bed and carry rock away.

00:04:39.466 --> 00:04:44.053
In a cross-section, this often produces a V-shaped valley with sloping sides.

00:04:44.283 --> 00:04:48.912
The chapter names the Rhine Valley and Damodar Valley as river-valley examples.

00:04:49.142 --> 00:04:52.811
Look for the narrow bottom and the river that continues to erode it.

00:04:53.561 --> 00:04:55.183
A glacier is moving ice.

00:04:55.413 --> 00:05:00.895
It scrapes and widens a valley, producing a broader floor and steep sides that resemble a U.

00:05:01.125 --> 00:05:04.667
The textbook notes glacial valleys in the Alps and Himalayas.

00:05:04.897 --> 00:05:13.899
A V and a U are useful simplified cross-sections: real valleys vary, but the shape helps you connect an erosional agent to its result.

00:05:14.649 --> 00:05:16.825
A rift valley has another origin.

00:05:17.055 --> 00:05:22.922
Movement along roughly parallel faults lets a block of crust drop lower than the land beside it.

00:05:23.152 --> 00:05:28.421
The chapter gives the Narmada Valley in India and Nile Valley in Egypt as examples.

00:05:28.651 --> 00:05:35.606
In a diagram, draw two faults and the lowered middle; do not use river erosion to explain that particular structure.

00:05:37.106 --> 00:05:41.181
A plain is a broad, nearly flat area of relatively low land.

00:05:41.411 --> 00:05:46.552
The textbook says plains are usually below about two hundred metres above sea level.

00:05:46.782 --> 00:05:53.609
Many large river plains formed from alluvium, the fine material deposited by rivers and their tributaries.

00:05:53.839 --> 00:05:58.084
But the chapter distinguishes three origins, not just river deposition.

00:05:58.834 --> 00:06:05.853
A structural plain can form when a broad section of the sea floor or continental shelf rises above sea level.

00:06:06.083 --> 00:06:09.240
Coastal sediments can later add to that flat surface.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:16.062
An erosional plain forms when wind, rivers, rain or ice wear higher land down over a long time.

00:06:16.292 --> 00:06:20.047
A very level erosion surface is sometimes called a peneplain.

00:06:20.797 --> 00:06:24.807
A depositional plain forms when transported sediment is laid down.

00:06:25.037 --> 00:06:31.032
Rivers build alluvial plains, while moving ice, wind and waves can also deposit material.

00:06:31.262 --> 00:06:37.385
Coastal plains occur beside seas; the eastern and western coastal plains of India are examples.

00:06:37.615 --> 00:06:42.244
For a comparison answer, use three verbs: uplift, wear down and lay down.

00:06:42.994 --> 00:06:47.005
Which process builds an alluvial plain by laying down river sediment?

00:06:55.235 --> 00:06:56.280
Deposition.

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:59.070
The resulting plain is a depositional plain.

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:06.301
Plains often support large populations because level land makes cultivation and building easier.

00:07:06.531 --> 00:07:11.843
River deposits can provide fertile soil for crops such as rice, wheat and sugar cane.

00:07:12.073 --> 00:07:19.859
The Indo-Gangetic, Mississippi and Yangtze Plains are examples of broad regions where agriculture and settlement have grown.

00:07:20.609 --> 00:07:27.415
Flat ground also makes it easier to build roads, railways and airports, and to expand towns and industries.

00:07:27.645 --> 00:07:31.527
That does not mean every plain is equally fertile or free from risk.

00:07:31.757 --> 00:07:39.565
Floods, water shortages and soil damage still matter; the benefits depend on water, climate and how people use the land.

00:07:40.315 --> 00:07:42.406
Now connect geography to history.

00:07:42.636 --> 00:07:50.743
Many ancient civilizations grew near river plains because water, farmland and transport routes could support large communities.

00:07:50.973 --> 00:07:55.709
A strong answer explains that chain rather than saying only that plains are flat.

00:07:55.939 --> 00:08:00.995
Compare it with a steep mountain slope: what changes for fields, roads and settlements?

00:08:02.495 --> 00:08:06.655
Three smaller landforms are easy to confuse until you draw the water.

00:08:06.885 --> 00:08:09.018
An island has water on all sides.

00:08:09.248 --> 00:08:17.397
Sri Lanka, Madagascar, Lakshadweep, and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands are examples in or near the Indian Ocean region.

00:08:17.627 --> 00:08:21.339
An island is separated from a larger land mass by water.

00:08:22.089 --> 00:08:26.697
A peninsula projects from a larger land mass and has water on three sides.

00:08:26.927 --> 00:08:35.610
India is a familiar example: the Arabian Sea lies to its west, the Bay of Bengal to its east, and the Indian Ocean to its south.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:38.443
The Malay Peninsula is another example.

00:08:38.673 --> 00:08:42.449
The land connection at the back is what distinguishes it from an island.

00:08:43.199 --> 00:08:49.300
An isthmus is a narrow strip of land with water on both sides that joins two larger land masses.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:55.674
The Isthmus of Panama links North and South America; the Isthmus of Suez links Africa and Asia.

00:08:55.904 --> 00:09:02.325
Use a simple sketch: island detached, peninsula attached and projecting, isthmus a thin connector.

00:09:03.075 --> 00:09:07.406
Which landform joins two larger land masses with a narrow strip of land?

00:09:15.636 --> 00:09:18.068
An isthmus, such as Panama or Suez.

00:09:19.048 --> 00:09:21.821
Landforms influence where people live and work.

00:09:22.051 --> 00:09:24.163
Height often brings cooler conditions.

00:09:24.393 --> 00:09:30.943
Steep slopes make roads, houses and large fields harder to build; flatter plains usually make these easier.

00:09:31.173 --> 00:09:33.797
Rocks and soils affect building and farming.

00:09:34.027 --> 00:09:41.728
Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions can cause serious harm, so the benefits of a landscape must be considered with its hazards.

00:09:42.478 --> 00:09:44.377
India gives several examples.

00:09:44.607 --> 00:09:53.119
The Himalayas form a major northern barrier and influence climate, while passes have provided routes for travellers, trade and movements of people.

00:09:53.349 --> 00:09:56.826
Coasts have connected seafaring communities with other regions.

00:09:57.056 --> 00:10:01.856
Rivers and fertile plains helped settled life and ancient civilizations grow.

00:10:02.606 --> 00:10:07.875
The Deccan Plateau, Vindhyas and Satpuras also shaped routes across the subcontinent.

00:10:08.105 --> 00:10:15.359
These are connections between geography and history, not claims that a single mountain or river decided everything.

00:10:15.589 --> 00:10:24.143
The chapter asks us to conserve landforms too: mining, roads and dams change them, so careful use matters for habitats and people.

00:10:25.643 --> 00:10:28.331
Finish with four questions for every landform.

00:10:28.561 --> 00:10:29.671
What is its shape?

00:10:29.901 --> 00:10:31.223
What process made it?

00:10:31.453 --> 00:10:32.989
Where is a named example?

00:10:33.219 --> 00:10:34.670
How does it affect life?

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:37.545
Try this for a plateau, a valley and a plain.

00:10:37.775 --> 00:10:42.959
Use the diagrams before looking at your notes; explain the arrows and labels out loud.

00:10:43.709 --> 00:10:52.435
For plateaus, compare tectonic uplift with lava flows and identify dissected, intermontane and piedmont by appearance or position.

00:10:52.665 --> 00:10:59.086
For valleys, distinguish V-shaped river erosion, U-shaped glacial erosion and a rift between faults.

00:10:59.316 --> 00:11:02.772
For plains, explain uplift, wearing down or deposition.

00:11:03.522 --> 00:11:09.685
Finally, use a map to locate a plateau, a river plain, an island, a peninsula and an isthmus.

00:11:09.915 --> 00:11:15.739
If you can define, draw, locate and explain each, you have revised this chapter in detail.

00:11:15.969 --> 00:11:20.683
Return to a question you missed and retry it before reading the exercise answer.

00:11:21.433 --> 00:11:24.911
Why is a plateau different from a plain if both can look flat?

00:11:33.141 --> 00:11:40.885
A plateau is raised above surrounding land, often with steep edges; a plain is broad, nearly level and relatively low.
